# 22. C Interop and libraries

Goal: call any external library (dll, lib, so, any format) that exposes a C-style interface.
Draft, not final. Example: Specs/draft_zlib.dflib.

## ALREADY IN THE SPEC
- Markers (13.1): plain = read-only lend, & = writable, -> = give, x! = copy,
  @ share, % weak, * raw pointer, &* writable raw pointer.
- extern fun, minimal (15.9): numbers, bool, addr, string parameter as const char*,
  arr<T> / &arr<T> as pointer + size_t, layout-tagged structs by copy or by *S / &*S.
- Structs (11.1.5): struct<fixed> / struct<compact> for C layout (11.1.5.6), plain
  values only; transmute (11.1.5.7) gives views over the same memory.
- string? and string(addr) / string(->addr) for C strings (4.4.6).
- toCStr(StringEncoding) gives a C string (UTF8, UTF16, ...) (4.3).

## LIBRARIES AND IMPORT
- Each C library has a Dflat wrapper file, `.dflib`, which holds all the library needs so the
  compiler removes the boilerplate: library name, functions, calling conventions, marshalling.
  It replaces the gfx setup hardcoded in the compiler.
- A program imports a library with `import lib <name>` (`import lib zlib`). The namespace of
  the library (its `Namespace:`) is imported with it: its public names are used without the
  prefix (`messageBox(...)`), and the fully qualified names stay valid.
- The namespace is imported only in the file that writes `import lib`. The library is part of
  the whole program: the other files use its fully qualified names (`std.win32.user32.messageBox(...)`)
  or import its namespace (`import std.win32.user32`, 16.2).
- A target is OS + CPU (the --target names).
- The location of the library files is in the .dsprj, key `libpath`: the library path where
  the files are found, possibly with a subfolder for each target.
- Loading a library by name while the program runs is in the standard library:
  `Root.Process.loadLib(name)`.

## DFLIB FILE
- Comments as in Dflat: /* comment */ and // comment.
- Name: <name> - the name used by `import lib`. No spaces. Mandatory.
- Namespace: <namespace> - the namespace of the native members, so Dflat code can fully
  qualify them (`std.compress.zlib`).
- Description: <text> - a short single-line description. Optional.
- Version: <text> - a string, since versions follow no single rule. Optional.
- Convention: <name> - the default calling convention of the library.
- Library level values are defaults; a function overrides them only where it differs.
- A calling convention in square brackets before a `native` overrides `Convention:` for that
  function: `[cdecl] native i32 wsprintfW(...)` in a stdcall library.

### Library files
- LibName, <target>, <kind>: <files> - the file names of the library binary for a target
  (no path). The target can use `*` as in the target blocks. The kind is `static` or `dynamic`:
  - `static: <file>` - linked into the executable.
  - `dynamic: <file>` - one file to link and to run (.so, .dylib).
  - `dynamic: <link file>, <run file>` - the import library to link and the library to run
    (Windows .lib + .dll); the compiler copies the run file next to the executable.
  - A run file in square brackets is not copied: it is part of the system (`[user32.dll]`).
```
LibName, linux-*, dynamic: libz.so
LibName, win-*, dynamic: zlib1.lib, zlib1.dll
```
- When both kinds are available, the import picks one: `import lib static zlib`,
  `import lib dynamic zlib`. With no kind, static is used, with a level 1 warning.

### Versions
- Two .dflib files can have the same LibName with different versions (zlib 1.2.3, zlib
  2.0beta); `import lib zlib 2.0beta` picks one (`import lib static zlib 2.0beta` with a kind).
- `import lib zlib` with no version when more than one version exists is a compile error.
- Two versions can be imported in the same program when their namespaces differ; when their
  members conflict it is a compile error.

### Functions
- Each C function is declared with `native` and its C types; the Dflat function that maps to
  it follows right after it. The Dflat function maps with `->`: its named results are
  assigned from the native call, so each item is mapped explicitly. The pairs are kept
  together so the file stays readable:
```
// get the zlib version
native *char zlibVersion()
fun string v version() -> v = string(zlibVersion())

// the largest size compress can produce from sourceLen bytes
native u32 compressBound(u32 sourceLen)
fun uns n bound(uns len) -> n = compressBound(len)

// decompress a buffer
native i32 uncompress(&*u8 dest, &*u32 destLen, *u8 source, u32 sourceLen)
fun i32 res, uns written uncompress(&arr<byte> dest, arr<byte> src) -> {
    u32 len = dest.len               // in: the size of the buffer
    res = uncompress(dest, &len, src, src.len)
    written = len                    // out: the bytes zlib wrote
}
```
- A function with no result maps to the call alone: `fun f() -> native_f()`.
- In the native call, `=` marks whatever is given back as output: `=name` is an out parameter
  that fills the named result (numbers, addr, handles alike):
```
native i32 callMe(i32 param, &*i32 res)
fun i32 res callMe(i32 param) -> callMe(param, =res)
```
- `null` is valid for a pointer parameter (NULL in C).
- C type names are written with an alias (16) or as the Dflat type of the same size, and a
  cast converts them in the mapping. Win32 BOOL is a 4-byte int, not a C bool:
```
alias i32 as BOOL
native BOOL Win_IsItWorking()                // or: native i32 Win_IsItWorking()
fun bool r isItWorking() -> r = (bool)Win_IsItWorking()
```
- `->` defines a mapping in a .dflib. When the call needs more than one line, the mapping is
  a block in braces; its locals live until the end of the block:
```
fun MsgBoxResult res messageBox(string text, string caption, MsgBoxButtons buttons, MsgBoxIcon icon)
  -> {
    var textBuf = text.toCStr(StringEncoding.UTF16)
    var captionBuf = caption.toCStr(StringEncoding.UTF16)
    res = [MsgBoxResult]MessageBoxW(null, (*u16)pointer.to(textBuf), (*u16)pointer.to(captionBuf),
                                    [u32]buttons | [u32]icon)
  }
```
- The native call places each length where C wants it (before, after, through a
  pointer), with no position annotations.
- `native` functions are not exposed to the program: it sees only the Dflat functions. Inside
  a mapping, a name that is both a `fun` and a `native` calls the native.
- A `native` whose types all match 1 to 1 is mapped automatically: the program calls it with
  its name, with no `fun`. The `native` line is written anyway: IDEs read the .dflib for
  context.
- The inline form (`-> native ...` on one line) is for 1 to 1 mappings; a mapping that needs
  more declares the `native` on its own line, as above. Variables use the same two forms.
- A function has an alias only when its C name is not a valid Dflat name.
- C int is i32 (not the Dflat int). Types whose size changes per target (C long) are written
  with their real size in target blocks (u32 by default, u64 on linux-x64 / linux-arm64).
- C constants (#define, enum values) become Dflat `const`; groups of related values (flags
  like the Win32 MB_*) become a Dflat enum, so a wrong value cannot be given.

### Variadic functions
- The variadic part of a C function (`...`) is a Dflat variadic parameter (13.4) whose type
  is a set (6.10) of the accepted types: each argument can be any type of the set, so one call
  can mix them; any other type is a compile error.
```
Generic CArg { i8, i16, i32, i64, u8, u16, u32, u64, r32, r64, addr, string }

native i32 printf(*char format, CArg args...)
fun i32 n print(string format, CArg args...) -> n = printf(format, args)

print("%s is %d years old, %.2f m\n", name, 42, 1.80)      // program
```
- The compiler writes a plain C call with the type of each argument; clang applies the C
  variadic rules (float to double, small integers to int, the stack on Apple arm64).
- A string argument is given as a C text for the call.
- The format string is not checked against the arguments: a mismatch is the caller's
  responsibility.

### Variables
- Exported C variables live in the namespace of the .dflib, as its functions do:
```
import lib libc                          // Namespace: std.lib.c
Console.log(std.lib.c.timezone)
```
- A plain type is read-only, `&` is read/write, as the passing markers (13.1).
- When the mapping is 1 to 1, the native is declared inline after `->`:
```
i32 countedTotal -> native i32 countedTotal          // read-only
&i32 writableVar -> native i32 writableVar           // read/write
```
- When the mapping needs more, the native is declared on its own line and mapped with `->`,
  as for functions:
```
i32 timezone -> native i32 timezone

win-* {
  native i32 _get_timezone(&*i32 seconds)
  i32 timezone -> _get_timezone(=timezone)           // read through a function
}
```

### Handles
- A C handle (an opaque pointer like sqlite3* or HWND, a void* HANDLE, an integer like a
  POSIX fd or a Vulkan VkBuffer, a small struct) is declared with `handle` in the .dflib, in
  its namespace. The declaration always says what the handle is made of:
```
handle HWND addr
handle Db addr
handle Buffer u64                                    // a Vulkan non-dispatchable handle
handle File i32 invalid -1                           // an invalid value other than null
handle Complex struct { i32 a, u64 b, r64 c, addr d }
```
- Each handle is its own type: a `Db` cannot be given where a `Buffer` is expected, and an
  `addr` cannot be given where a `Db` is expected. A handle can be used where the type it is
  made of is expected (`string(mem)` with `handle CoMem addr`).
- `string(mem)` of a handle borrows the text (4.4.6): the handle is linked to the string and
  is not freed until the string ends.
- The C string forms of 4.4.6 take a StringEncoding, UTF8 by default: `string(addr,
  StringEncoding encoding = StringEncoding.UTF8)`, `string(->addr, StringEncoding encoding =
  StringEncoding.UTF8)`. UTF8 works as 4.4.6 says (no copy). Any other encoding reads the text
  in that encoding and copies it into a new string: the address is not kept, so a handle given
  to it can be freed right away, and `string(->addr, ...)` frees the C text at once.
- `string(addr!)` (4.4.6) copies the C text into a new string at once and never frees
  it: for a text the library keeps and frees itself (sqlite3_column_text is freed at the next
  sqlite3_step). Inside `string(...)`, `addr!` means "copy the text", as `->addr` means "take
  it"; it takes a StringEncoding too: `string(addr!, StringEncoding.UTF16)`.
```
fun string p documentsPath() -> {
    CoMem mem
    SHGetKnownFolderPath(FOLDERID_Documents, 0, null, &mem)
    p = string(mem, StringEncoding.UTF16)            // a copy
}                                                    // mem ends: CoTaskMemFree
```
- A handle has no arithmetic. It is stored, passed, returned, compared with a handle of the
  same type, and compared with `null`, which means its invalid value; a struct handle
  is the exception (below).
- A handle cannot be copied, only moved (`->`, 13.1): a copy (`h!`, `clone()`) is a compile
  error, so only one owner can free it. Plain and `&` parameters lend it as usual.
- A handle given with `->` (`f(->h)`) belongs to the function: the variable becomes null, and
  the freeing function of the handle runs as soon as that function ends (unless it moves the
  handle on).
- Used where the type it is made of is expected, a handle is lent (`string(mem)`: the handle
  still frees it) or moved (`string(->mem)`: the text is copied, then the freeing function of
  the handle runs at once, CoTaskMemFree for a CoMem).
- A struct handle is never null and cannot be compared with `null`: C gives a struct either
  by value (a real struct, never null) or as a pointer (`Foo*` is a `pointer<Foo>`, nullable
  like every pointer, 12.1).
- A native call creates it, usually through an out parameter:
  `native i32 sqlite3_open(*char name, &Db db)`.
- The out parameter fills a named result with `=` (Functions); the native result can be
  assigned to another named result:
```
handle Ref addr
native i32 native_get_ctx(*char name, &Ref ctx)
fun Ref r, i32 res getContext(string name) -> res = native_get_ctx(name, =r)
```
- Several out parameters fill several named results:
  `fun Res window, Res screen gfxScreens(string name) -> nativegfxcall(name, =window, =screen)`.
- The function that frees a handle is a native + fun pair, marked with `[~T name]` on its
  parameter; one per handle type (a second one is a compile error):
```
handle Db addr

// close a database
native i32 sqlite3_close(Db db)
fun i32 res close([~Db db]) -> res = sqlite3_close(db)
```
- The program can call it (`i32 res = close(db)`); the handle becomes null after it, so it is
  not freed again when the variable ends.
- When an owned handle ends and it is not null, the compiler calls the freeing function and
  ignores its result. A null handle is never given to it.
- A freeing function with more than one parameter (vkDestroyBuffer(device, buffer,
  pAllocator)) is never called automatically: the handle must be freed by an explicit call.

### Targets
- The items that differ per target go in a block; the Dflat side must be the same in every
  block. A definition outside any block is the default; a block that matches the target
  replaces it for that target. There is no `* { }` block.
- A block can name several targets, separated by commas, and use `*` as a wildcard:
```
linux-x64, osx-arm64, win-x32 {
  // valid for all the targets listed
}
*-x64 {
  // all x64 targets
}
linux-* {
  // all linux architectures
}
```
- When several blocks match, the most specific one wins; between equally specific blocks
  (`linux-*` and `*-x64` on linux-x64) the first one in the file wins.
- A function or variable that has no definition for a target is an error only when it is used
  in a program built for that target.

## CALLING CONVENTIONS
- One convention per target: win-x64 (Microsoft x64; __stdcall/__cdecl/__fastcall ignored,
  only __vectorcall differs), linux-x64 / osx-x64 (System V), arm64 (AAPCS64; Apple differs
  only for variadic arguments, a target rule).
- win-x32 is where it matters: cdecl (C default), stdcall (Win32 API, WINAPI/CALLBACK, names
  decorated _Name@<bytes>), fastcall (rare), vectorcall (rare); thiscall is C++ only.
- ARM32 hard/soft float is per target, never per function.
- Dflat compiles to C: the compiler writes the keyword in the prototype and the wrapper,
  clang does the rest (decoration included).
- Callbacks need the convention too (WNDPROC is stdcall).
- A convention is ignored on targets where it means nothing, as C compilers do.

Examples:
```
// zlib (zlib1.dll, libz.so): every function is cdecl
Name: zlib
Convention: cdecl
native u32 crc32(u32 crc, *u8 buf, u32 len)                 // cdecl
```
```
// user32: the Win32 API is stdcall
Name: user32
Convention: stdcall
native i32 MessageBoxW(Hwnd owner, *u16 text, *u16 caption, u32 type)    // stdcall
native i32 GetSystemMetrics(i32 index)                                    // stdcall
```
```
// a stdcall library with one cdecl function
Name: mixed
Convention: stdcall
native i32 openDevice(i32 id)                               // stdcall
[cdecl] native i32 deviceVersion()                          // cdecl
```
- On win-x32 cdecl and stdcall produce different calls; on win-x64, linux-x64 and arm64
  they produce the same call.

## OWNERSHIP / WHO FREES
- C headers and libraries do not express it; it is in the manual at best. Nothing is assumed.
- Memory that the caller must free with a specific function is a handle too, with the same
  rules (Handles): one handle type per freeing function.
```
handle CoMem addr                                    // memory from the COM allocator

native CoTaskMemFree(CoMem p)
fun free([~CoMem p]) -> CoTaskMemFree(p)

native i32 SHGetKnownFolderPath(*u8 id, u32 flags, addr token, &CoMem path)
fun CoMem path, i32 res knownFolder(*u8 id, u32 flags) -> res = SHGetKnownFolderPath(id, flags, null, =path)
```
- string(->addr) on a plain addr frees with C free. Memory with its own freeing function
  (sqlite3_free, CoTaskMemFree) is a handle, and string(->h) frees it with that function.

## TOOLS (later)
- A tool that reads a .h and writes a first .dflib, only what is possible; the .dflib is then
  tuned by hand, and the tool is used again only for a new library version (no merge).
- A minimal in-house C preprocessor, with clang for complex cases (clang -E -dD keeps macro
  definitions); clang output depends on the target.
- Calling Dflat libraries from C (exports, a generated .h): out of scope for now.

## POINTERS (12.1)
- Shared objects (z_stream): the C struct is a struct<fixed> in the .dflib, its pointer
  fields are pointer<T> / pointer<&T> holding the C pointer only, with no bounds and no
  validity guarantee. The wrapper hides them where it can (compress(in, &out) sets
  nextIn / nextOut before each deflate); no miracles: what C gives without a size stays
  unbounded.
  ```
  struct<fixed> ZStream {
    pointer<u8>  nextIn       // const Bytef*
    u32          availIn
    u64          totalIn
    pointer<&u8> nextOut      // Bytef*
    u32          availOut
    u64          totalOut
    pointer<u8>  msg          // char*, set by zlib
    addr         state        // zlib's own memory, opaque
    ...
  }
  ```
- A .dflib is not unsafe as a whole: only the methods that need it are marked `unsafe`.
- Returned arrays (Vulkan two-call): the second call takes `pointer.to(&devs)` (`&*devs[0]`),
  bounded to the array and alive for the call only; the wrapper may hide the two calls.
- Wide strings: a pointer cast, `(*u16)pointer.to(text.toCStr(StringEncoding.UTF16))`;
  the count becomes bytes / 2.
- C unions: no union declaration; the same bytes are read as another type with transmute
  (3.5).
- C errors (errno, GetLastError, result codes) never become exceptions by themselves: the
  wrapper code reads them and throws an exception when it wants to.

## CALLBACKS
- A callback is a pointer to a function with the signature the C side expects; the .dflib
  declares the callback type. The type of the pointer is `pointer<fun(...)>`.
```
fun iAmACallback(i32 a, *arr<char> b) {
  ...
}
pointer funPointer = pointer.to(iAmACallback(i32, *arr<char>))   // pointer<fun(i32, *arr<char>)>
```
- The parameter types in `pointer.to(f(...))` choose the function among its overloads.
- C never passes an array by value: an array parameter is written `*arr<char>` or
  `pointer<char>`.
- Only plain functions and static methods; lambdas with captured state (through the C
  `void* userData`) come later.
- The calling convention comes from the callback type in the .dflib: the compiler writes a
  small C function with that convention that calls the Dflat function; the Dflat function
  has no tag.

